{"text": "Sofia learns most successfully through visual schedule and worked example; explicit teaching of hidden expectations; choice within predictable routines; and short practice followed by movement. These strategies make instruction clearer and reduce the amount of energy spent simply gaining access.\n\nThe profile also identifies predictable barriers. Sofia may struggle with unstructured group work; figurative or vague directions; fire alarms and cafeteria noise; and shifting away from a preferred topic. We should not treat these points as a list of personal deficits. Each one directs the team toward a response. For example, a barrier caused by pace may require additional processing time; an environmental barrier may require a quieter, safer, or more accessible setting; and task complexity may require modeling, chunking, direct instruction, or an alternate response.\n\nThe goal is not to remove every productive challenge. The goal is to remove barriers that prevent the student from engaging with the intended learning. Presenter 3 will now connect the profile’s sensory, medical, behavioural, and safety information to the EA’s day-to-day responsibilities.", "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural", "audio": "special-education-G-04.mp3", "words": [{"text": "Sofia", "start": 0.125, "end": 0.6375, "start_char": 0, "end_char": 5}, {"text": "learns", "start": 0.65, "end": 1.0125, "start_char": 6, "end_char": 12}, {"text": "most", "start": 1.025, "end": 1.2875, "start_char": 13, "end_char": 17}, {"text": "successfully", "start": 1.3, "end": 1.9875, "start_char": 18, "end_char": 30}, {"text": "through", "start": 2.0, "end": 2.2375, "start_char": 31, "end_char": 38}, {"text": "visual", "start": 2.25, "end": 2.6125, "start_char": 39, "end_char": 45}, {"text": "schedule", "start": 2.625, "end": 3.15, "start_char": 46, "end_char": 54}, {"text": "and", "start": 3.1625, "end": 3.275, "start_char": 55, "end_char": 58}, {"text": "worked", "start": 3.2875, "end": 3.6125, "start_char": 59, 